The Linux `top` common is used to monitor system performance.
It shows the processes currently run on a Linux server.
It is very useful for Linux administrators and DevOps engineers.
The `PID` column means Process ID.
Every run process has a unique PID number.
The `USER` column shows the user who started the process.
The `PR` column means process priority.
The `NI` column shows the nice value of the process.
The `VIRT` column means virtual memory used by the process.
The `RES` column means resident memory.
RES shows the actual RAM being used by the process.
The `SHR` column means shared memory.
Shared memory can be used by multiple processes.
The `S` column shows the current process status.
`R` means the process is run.
`S` means the process is sleeping.
`D` means the process is in uninterruptible sleep.
`T` means the process is stopped.
`Z` means the process is a zombie process.
The `%CPU` column shows CPU usage.
High CPU usage can make a server slow.
The `%MEM` column shows memory usage percentage.
High memory usage can cause memory problems.
The `TIME+` column shows total CPU time used.
The `COMMAND` column shows the run common or application.
For example, the common can be `java`, `nginx`, or `docker`.
In real-time troubleshooting, first check CPU usage.
Then check memory usage.
After that, check the process PID.
The PID helps us identify a specific process.
We can use `ps -fp PID` to get more process information.
For example, `ps -fp 2456` checks process 2456.
We can use `kill PID` to stop a process gracefully.
For example, `kill 2456` sends a termination signal.
We should not kill an important process without checking it.
The `top` common also helps identify resource-heavy applications.
A Java application may consume high CPU during a build.
A database process may consume high memory during heavy queries.
Docker-related processes can also appear in the output.
DevOps engineers commonly use `top` during server troubleshooting.
It helps identify CPU and memory bottlenecks.
It can also help identify unexpected processes.
The most important columns are PID, USER, CPU, MEM, STATUS, and COMMAND.
